[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 821,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 764,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125230,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 85,
        "headingId": 764,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Proliferative nodules (PNs) are benign nodular proliferations of melanocytes arising in medium or giant congenital naevi (GCNs).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:14:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 822,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 765,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125231,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 85,
        "headingId": 765,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8762/1 Proliferative nodules in congenital melanocytic naevus</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:14:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 823,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 766,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125232,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 85,
        "headingId": 766,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2F20.2 &amp; XH6AH3 Congenital melanocytic naevus &amp; Proliferative dermal lesion in congenital naevus</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:14:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 824,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 767,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125233,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 85,
        "headingId": 767,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> expansile and cellular nodules; atypical proliferative nodules.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:14:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 825,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 768,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125234,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 85,
        "headingId": 768,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:14:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 826,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 769,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125235,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 85,
        "headingId": 769,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>PNs occur in the same distribution as GCNs, particularly the trunk {25517953}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:14:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 827,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 770,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125236,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 85,
        "headingId": 770,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>PNs are pink, brown, blue, or black papules, plaques, or nodules within a congenital naevus. They are typically &lt;&nbsp;20&nbsp;mm in size and can be ulcerated {25517953}. They may be present at birth or arise later and show rapid growth {27486690}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:14:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 828,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 771,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125237,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 85,
        "headingId": 771,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>PNs arise in 3&ndash;19% of GCNs {21923752; 22671135; 1593350}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:14:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 829,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 772,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125238,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 85,
        "headingId": 772,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:14:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 830,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 773,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125239,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 85,
        "headingId": 773,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Secondary genomic aberrations such as mutations, chromosomal rearrangements, or copy-number changes in a congenital naevus lead to expansion of a new clone {25517953; 27486690; 27849631; 12368190; 26763461}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:14:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 837,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 780,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125240,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 85,
        "headingId": 780,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>See <em>Clinical features</em>, above.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:14:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 831,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 774,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125241,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 85,
        "headingId": 774,
        "headingText": "<p>Within the dermal or subcutaneous portion of a congenital naevus there is an expansile proliferation of melanocytes with spitzoid, small blue round cell, epithelioid, or spindle morphology {25517953}. The morphological transition from the surrounding naevus cells typically is gradual with intermediate cellular morphologies, but it can be abrupt {14690460}. The mitotic count is typically &lt;&nbsp;2&nbsp;mitoses/mm<sup>2</sup>, and cells lack high-grade nuclear atypia. Cases with small blue round cell morphology may have more mitoses.</p>\r\n<h5>Immunohistochemistry</h5>\r\n<p>Immunomarkers for melanocytes, such as melan-A, tyrosinase, and SOX10, are expressed in PNs. Loss of p16 or H3 p.K27me3 expression and increased labelling with Ki-67 raise concern for melanoma.</p>\r\n<h5>Differential diagnosis</h5>\r\n<p>Criteria favouring melanoma are sharply demarcated, expansive nodules; necrosis; nuclear enlargement; pleomorphism; hyperchromasia; prominent nucleoli; numerous mitotic figures; and atypical mitoses {14690460}. Both melanoma arising in a congenital naevus and PNs may express PRAME, so its usefulness is unclear.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:14:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 832,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 775,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125242,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 85,
        "headingId": 775,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:14:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 833,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 776,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125243,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 85,
        "headingId": 776,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Gains or losses of entire chromosomes are seen in PNs, whereas melanomas tend to have segmental copy-number aberrations {12368190}. Mutations or epigenetic modification of the <em>TERT</em> promoter favour a diagnosis of melanoma {26763461}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:14:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 836,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 779,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125244,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 85,
        "headingId": 779,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> a benign expansile clone of melanocytes within a GCN.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> normal expression of H3 p.K27me3.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:14:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 834,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 777,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125245,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 85,
        "headingId": 777,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:14:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 835,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 778,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125246,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 85,
        "headingId": 778,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>There is currently no evidence that PNs progress to melanoma, and nodules tend to spontaneously stop growing and often regress. Local recurrences may occur, showing greater cytological atypia.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:14:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]